  "worked_solution": "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }\u2026\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},\u2026,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
